 one particle in 1d we can now consider  the virial theorem for a multi-particle  system described by a potential that is  a homogeneous function of degree k  we have that k times the expectation  value of the potential energy operator  is equal to two times the expectation  value of the kinetic energy operator  let's consider two quick examples  the first is for the hydrogen atom which  is a two particle system in 3d  we have a full series on the hydrogen  atom and i recommend you check it out by  following the relevant links in the  description but what matters for our  purposes today is that the potential  energy of the hydrogen atom is given by  the electrostatic interaction between a  proton  and an electron  and it takes its usual form  note that here we're considering a  non-relativistic hydrogen atom  spelling this out we see that the  potential energy is a function of six  variables  and explicitly we can write it out in  this long form  feel free to pause here to cross check  this but all we've done is to spell out  the distance between the proton and  electron in the denominator  moving on with this expression it's easy  to confirm that the potential function  obeys this relation when we multiply the  arguments by scalar s  the exponent here  means that this is a homogeneous  function of degree minus one  now we haven't done this explicitly but  it's a good exercise so feel free to try  it out  but now we come to the key point  without the need to find the hydrogen  atom eigenvalues or eigenstates we can  immediately use the viral theorem to  write that for any energy eigenstate the  expectation value of the potential  energy  is equal to -2 times the expectation  value of the kinetic energy  now the hydrogen atom together with the  harmonic oscillator is one of the very  few quantum mechanical systems for which  we have a full analytical solution  in fact in the video link in the  description on the ground state of the  hydrogen atom we explicitly calculate  these expectation values and confirm the  variable theorem  however to get to this result using the  full solution is a rather tedious  process as solving the hydrogen atom  eigenvalue problem isn't trivial by  contrast using the visual theorem we can  get to this same conclusion without any  knowledge of the precise form of the  hydrogen atom eigenvalues or  eigenfunctions  let's make some room  and consider a second example which will  be a very general quantum system an atom  a molecule or a material  all these systems are made of a single  atom or of a collection of atoms  in turn these atoms are made of a  collection of atomic nuclei and  electrons  however  irrespective of how complex the system  is or of how many particles it has  the potential energy terms always have  the same form  ignoring relativistic effects again the  component particles all interact by an  electrostatic interaction an interaction  v between any two particles one and two  takes the same general form  we have the usual pre-factor  and then the product of the electric  charges  over the distance between the two  particles  as this form is the same we had earlier  between interacting proton and electron  in a hydrogen atom we can immediately  conclude that these tubal interaction  terms obey this relation  and are therefore homogeneous functions  of degree minus one  the total potential energy of a  multi-particle system is the sum of the  individual two body terms so the total  potential energy is also a homogeneous  function of degree minus one  we can therefore use the variable  theorem to conclude that for any  eigenstate of any of these systems the  expectation value of the potential  energy  is equal to -2 times the expectation  value of the kinetic energy  this is a very general result and again  we have been able to obtain it without  having to first solve the eigenvalue  equation of the hamiltonian to determine  the eigenvalues or eigenstates  unlike hydrogen for any of these systems  it is impossible to exactly calculate  the energy eigenvalues or eigenstates  the virial theorem then tells us that  whatever form the unknown solution of  the problem takes  it must obey these relation  the virial theorem is a very neat  mathematical result that allows us to  understand some key properties of any  quantum system even if we don't have a  full mathematical solution as a  follow-up i recommend that you check out  the video on the ground state of the  hydrogen atom in that video we can  demonstrate analytically that the video  theorem is satisfied and as always if  you like the video please subscribe
 

